From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/a733: kms: advertise ARGB8888, which the layer already scans

Every GBM/EGL client that asks for an alpha channel gets AR24, and on
this driver addfb then rejects the whole frame. Measured on the 8 GiB board on
2026-09-08 with glmark2 on a test build of this series: the GPU renders at 1920x1200
through zink on the PowerVR, 166-170 FPS, and not one frame reaches
the screen --

  sun60i-a733-kms display-engine: [drm:drm_gem_fb_init_with_funcs]
    Unsupported pixel format AR24 little-endian (0x34325241) / modifier 0x0
  sun60i-a733-kms display-engine: [drm:drm_internal_framebuffer_create]
    could not create framebuffer

repeated once per frame, surfacing in userspace as "Failed to create FB:
-22" and "Failed to get gbm front buffer". Note the modifier in that line: 0x0
is DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR, which we do advertise. LINEAR was never the problem.
The format list was, and it had exactly one entry.

WHY THIS COSTS NO NEW REGISTER VALUE, WHICH IS THE ONLY REASON IT IS ALLOWED

`drm-a733-kms-drm-device-for-the-display-pipe`'s own comment argues against
adding formats, and the argument is right: "advertising a format the layer
would program wrong is worse than advertising one". ARGB8888 does not trip
it, and that is a property of the hardware programming rather than a
preference:

  - sun60i_de_attctl() takes no arguments. It returns one constant word,
    0xff008403, decoded field by field: global alpha [31:24] = 0xff,
    RGB_MODE bit 15, FBFMT [12:8] = 4, ALPHA_MODE [2:1] = 01, EN.
  - ALPHA_MODE 01 is global/layer alpha. The layer takes its alpha from the
    [31:24] field, which is 0xff, and never reads the pixel's fourth byte.
  - AR24 and XR24 are the same 32-bit little-endian layout; they differ only
    in what that fourth byte means, and this layer does not look at it.

So the same word scans an AR24 buffer and an XR24 buffer identically. This
patch adds a name to the list. It does not add a code path, and it writes no
value this tree has not already had on silicon.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO

  - No per-pixel blending. On a single primary plane over an opaque BKCOLOR
    there is nothing to blend against, so there is no alpha meaning to lose
    here -- but a second plane, or a non-opaque background, has to revisit
    it.
  - No new modifier, no scaling, no positioning, no second format family.
    The other three restrictions in that comment stand untouched.

FALSIFIERS, in the order they answer

  1. The two drm_dbg_kms lines above must not appear for AR24 any more, with
     drm.debug=0x04 and the same glmark2 invocation.
  2. glmark2 must stop printing "Failed to create FB: -22" -- zero
     occurrences, not fewer.
  3. A PICTURE, and this is the one that matters: glmark2 must appear on the
     monitor in place of the fbcon login. Witness is the TCON's own scanout
     verb, never fb0/blank and never dpms, plus an eye on the screen.
     ⚠ The console on that monitor is fbcon and it shares card0, so the
     first cut of this header required vtcon1 unbound first. THE BOARD
     SAYS OTHERWISE: vtcon1 stayed bound, read back as 1, and glmark2
     took the DRM master with fbcon stepping aside by itself.
     `drm-a733-kms-drm-device-for-the-display-pipe`'s cmp
     proof needed the unbind because it compared a buffer fbcon was
     writing into -- a second writer, which is not the same thing as a
     client that owns the CRTC.
  4. The pixels must be right, not merely present: sink_crc must change
     between two different rendered pictures and hold constant across a
     static one, sampled as echo 0; echo 1; cat on sink_crc.
  5. XRGB8888 must still work exactly as before -- the console proof
     (16/16 cells) must still pass.
     Since 3 turns out not to need the unbind, 3 and 5 share one boot.
     ⚠ But 5 WRITES ITS OWN STRING into the framebuffer, so it leaves the
     screen changed. Run it after 4, never before, or 4 has no static
     picture left to measure.

✅ HW-PROVEN on the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-08, a test build of this series booted as its own extlinux
label, the real default untouched. F1 met: zero AR24 rejections, was one
per frame. F2 met: zero "Failed to create FB", was one per frame, glmark2
Score 164-168 at 1920x1200 through zink on the PowerVR. F3 met: glmark2's
horse on the screen, confirmed by eye, with the TCON scanning cleanly
underneath -- line counter 2..1231, 4 wraps, underflow 0, lostwarn 0.
F4a met: sink_crc 0x6553 -> 0x8687 -> 0x437d under a moving picture.
F5 met: console proof 16/16 cells, bit for bit, same boot.

⬜ F4b was not obtained, and BOTH attempts were the measurer's fault, not
the sink's. The first used the console as the "static" picture, and the
console has a blinking cursor. The second ran after falsifier 5, which
writes "A733 FBCON PROOF" into the framebuffer, and after a printf of the
measurer's own into the same tty -- both still on the screen when the
samples were taken, as confirmed by eye. So no static
picture ever existed to measure, twice over, and the wobbling values
(0x317f, 0xf149, 0x5d4f, 0xf149) say nothing about sink_crc. The
2026-08-26 reading of three identical values stands unchallenged.
⇒ Redo on a genuinely static screen, BEFORE falsifier 5, with the cursor
off on the controlling terminal rather than by redirection.

⚠ WHAT THIS FIXES AND WHAT IT LEAVES. addfb succeeds; the flip does not
always find a buffer. "Failed to get gbm front buffer" still appears 1642
times in 25 s at ~169 FPS -- about 39 % of frames. That is the vblank
half, not the format half: this driver calls no drm_vblank_init(), has no
.enable_vblank, and completes a flip with drm_atomic_helper_fake_vblank()
from the commit tail, so flip completion is decoupled from the real
scanout. A deliberate state of this tree, and the next thing to pick up.

Assisted-by: Claude

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
@@ -319,12 +319,31 @@
  * one of those restrictions is what `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` has on silicon and not one of them is
  * a placeholder for something that would work if asked:
  *
- *   - ONE FORMAT. sun60i_de_attctl() builds a single word with FBFMT =
- *     OVL_FBFMT_XRGB8888 and RGB_MODE set. The channel's format field is five
- *     bits wide and the DE can do many more, but each one is a different
- *     ATTCTL and, for YUV, a different CCSC matrix -- none of which has been on
- *     a board. Advertising a format the layer would program wrong is worse than
- *     advertising one.
+ *   - ONE LAYER PROGRAMMING, TWO NAMES FOR IT. sun60i_de_attctl() builds a
+ *     single word with FBFMT = OVL_FBFMT_XRGB8888 and RGB_MODE set. The
+ *     channel's format field is five bits wide and the DE can do many more,
+ *     but each one is a different ATTCTL and, for YUV, a different CCSC
+ *     matrix -- none of which has been on a board. Advertising a format the
+ *     layer would program wrong is worse than advertising one.
+ *
+ *     ARGB8888 is the one exception, and it is an exception because it costs
+ *     no new register value. It is XRGB8888's memory layout byte for byte, and
+ *     ATTCTL sets ALPHA_MODE = OVL_ALPHA_MODE_LAYER with a global alpha of
+ *     0xff, so the layer takes its alpha from that field and never reads the
+ *     pixel's fourth byte. The same word therefore scans an AR24 buffer and an
+ *     XR24 buffer identically -- this adds a name, not a code path.
+ *
+ *     ⚠ What it does NOT add is per-pixel blending. On a single primary plane
+ *     over an opaque BKCOLOR there is nothing to blend against, so alpha has
+ *     no meaning here to lose; a second plane would have to revisit this.
+ *     ⚠ Why it is needed rather than cosmetic: every GBM/EGL client that asks
+ *     for an alpha channel gets AR24, and addfb then fails the whole frame.
+ *     Measured on the 8 GiB board on 2026-09-08 with glmark2 -- the GPU rendered at
+ *     1920x1200 and every single frame died in
+ *     drm_gem_fb_init_with_funcs() with "Unsupported pixel format AR24
+ *     little-endian (0x34325241) / modifier 0x0", errno -22, so nothing ever
+ *     reached the screen. The modifier in that line is 0x0: LINEAR was never
+ *     the problem, the format list was.
  *   - LINEAR ONLY. TFBD (tiled-framebuffer decode) is left at reset by `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` and
  *     read back rather than written, so there is no compressed source to
  *     describe. DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR is therefore the complete list.
@@ -337,6 +356,7 @@
  */
 static const u32 sun60i_kms_plane_formats[] = {
 	DRM_FORMAT_XRGB8888,
+	DRM_FORMAT_ARGB8888,
 };
 
 static const u64 sun60i_kms_plane_modifiers[] = {
